\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY MARKETS-Dollar funding rate falls further in Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2009\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Dollar 3-month lending rate slides to another low\par \par \uc2 * LIBOR seen nearing bottom, pace of decline likely to slow\par \par \uc2 * New Zealand IRS down after soft producer price data\par \par \uc2 By Umesh Desai\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 18 (Reuters) - The cost of dollar funding in Asia slipped further on Monday extending its near 2 week falling streak while New Zealand swaps dipped on data that supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pledge to keep interest rates low.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, interbank rates remained soft as the banking system was flush from the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, interest rates on 3-month dollars fell to yet another record low of 0.81333 percent from Friday's 0.84714 percent, as global money market benchmarks tumbled.\par \par \uc2 Three-month dollar Libor slipped to 0.82563 percent on Friday, another record low, from 0.85438 percent on Thursday, and in the interest rate swaps market, the cost to exchange two-year fixed-rate dollars for floating-rate ones fell to their lowest since May 2007.\par \par \uc2 But some analysts say rates appear to be nearing bottom and a significant decline from these levels would need major pronouncements from policy makers.\par \par \uc2 "The LIBOR coming down is enticing people to return to markets but I don't see it (LIBOR) going down a lot more from these levels," said Suresh Ramanathan, a strategist with CIMB Bank.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets have rallied with the improvement in risk appetite as investors bet the global economy freefall has bottomed out. The MSCI index covering Asia ex-Japan has surged 53 percent from its 2009 low on March 4 to its yearly high on May 11.\par \par \uc2 "We need to see more really dovish comments from the central banks for LIBOR to come down a lot more and I don't see that happening," he said referring to Friday's comments from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet.\par \par \uc2 Late on Friday, Trichet said the measures taken by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate the ailing euro-zone economy can be removed quickly when the economy improves.\par \par \uc2 In New Zealand, data showed producer prices fell the quickest pace on record in the first quarter, lending support to expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep rates low.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand economy has been in recession since the beginning of 2008, the worst in more than 30 years, and forecasters expect the weakness to continue for much of 2009 because of the weak global outlook.\par \par \uc2 Swap rates were softer along the curve, with two year swaps at 3.36 percent and five-year swaps at 4.66 percent.\par \par \uc2 Swap rates have slowly eased back towards levels in late April when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to keep rates low until late next year. They spiked in March and early April as investors priced in an end to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easing cycle.\par \par \uc2 "So the inflationary burst we got from commodity prices is unwinding which is helpful, it allows the Reserve Bank to ease with less concern," said Robin Clements, senior economist with UBS.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, the three-month Hibor was fixed at 0.52143 percent -- its lowest level since early January 2005 and down 2 basis points from Friday.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity has remained flush in the local interbank market on frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the strength of the Hong Kong dollar and keep it within its trading band against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the aggregate balance -- the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the HKMA -- to a record HK$256.97 billion on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged at 7.80 to the U.S. dollar but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, the HKMA is usually obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hits 7.75 or 7.85 to keep the band intact.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Umesh Desai)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-MONEY (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090518e55i000ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
