\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Why is the Hong Kong dollar so strong?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Susan Fenton   \par \uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2009\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, May 8 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been injecting massive amounts of funds into the money market to stem an appreciating Hong Kong dollar <HKD=> and keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its fixed trading band with the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2  Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg system, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the local dollar hits the upper or lower limit of its trading band between 7.7500 and 7.8500 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2  By Friday the HKMA had sold HK$58.760 billion ($7.6 billion) for U.S. dollars since April 21. That brought the aggregate balance -- the sum of the balances of banks' clearing accounts with the HKMA -- to a record high HK$211.033 billion <HKMAOOC>.\par \par \uc2  That is a five-fold increase in the past six months and four times bigger than a 2004 peak when the peg came under speculative pressure. For a graphic see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/059/HK_DLR0509.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://graphics.thomsonreuters.com/059/HK_DLR0509.jpg}}}\par \par \uc2  Following are some questions and answers about the HKMA's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2  WHY IS THE \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  DOLLAR SO STRONG?\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trading at or close to the top of its band since last October as risk-averse investors saw it as a safe place to park funds amid the global financial crisis. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar and the HKMA last October guaranteed customer deposits at Hong Kong banks for two years.\par \par \uc2  As risk aversion receded, the Hong Kong dollar has again hit the top of its band as funds flow back into the territory, both into surging Hong Kong shares and as a place to park money before allocating it to other Asian markets.\par \par \uc2  Average turnover on the Hong Kong stock exchange has risen 60 percent in the past two months as the benchmark Hang Seng Index <.HSI> has rallied 51 percent to a seven-month high.\par \par \uc2  An upturn in China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a seven-month high this week has also helped the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2  WHAT ARE THE IMPLICATIONS FOR HONG KONG'S \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  PEG?\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg is not under pressure. There is no sign that speculators are buying the Hong Kong dollar to bet that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be de-pegged or its trading band widened.\par \par \uc2  Hong Kong government officials and the HKMA have repeatedly stressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg underpins financial stability, and market players appear to accept there will be no change.\par \par \uc2  WHAT ARE THE RISKS OF SUCH A JUMP IN THE AGGREGATE BALANCE?\par \par \uc2  Massive fund injections by the HKMA into the money market are similar to quantitative easing and are boosting market liquidity and pushing down Hong Kong interbank rates <HIBOR=R>.\par \par \uc2  Extra liquidity raises the risk of inflation, though analysts say inflationary pressure is low because Hong Kong's economy is still suffering from the hits to trade and the financial sector.\par \par \uc2  But liquidity does risk an asset price bubble by fuelling gains in equity and property prices. HKMA Chief Executive Joseph Yam said on Friday that heavy fund inflows into \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  could inflate asset prices. [ID:nHKG346756]\par \par \uc2  HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's strength poses little risk to Hong Kong's financial stability because the HKMA increases \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by selling Hong Kong dollars and buying U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2  CAN THE HKMA KEEP \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENING\b0 \highlight\uc2  INDEFINITELY?\par \par \uc2  In theory the HKMA has unlimited room to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has also been issuing Exchange Fund notes, which mop up excess liquidity in the market and reduce the aggregate balance, and is considering selling more bills, a move analysts say is aimed at sterilizing the proceeds of HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2  The HKMA said demand for Exchange Fund bills by banks was strong for liquidity management. Despite their low yields, banks prefer to hold the paper than maintain non-interest bearing balances in their clearing accounts, the HKMA said.\par \par \uc2  IS THE HK DOLLAR LIKELY TO REMAIN AT THE TOP OF ITS BAND?\par \par \uc2  Yes in the short term, as the territory is likely to keep attracting funds intended for the stock market and initial public offerings, with several new listings in the pipeline.\par \par \uc2  However, financial markets are riding on growing optimism that the worst is over for the global economy. If that view were to reverse, Hong Kong could quickly see an outflow of funds and downward pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Later in the year, analysts see the Hong Kong dollar falling back toward 7.80 to the U.S. dollar, arguing that carry trades -- where investors sell Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars to make a profit on the interest-rate differentials -- could resume.  (US$=HK$7.8)  (Reporting by Susan Fenton; Editing by Eric Burroughs & Kim Coghill)  (susan.fenton@thomsonreuters.com; +852 2843 6367; Reuters Messaging: susan.fenton.thomsonreuters.com@reuters.net)  \par \par \uc2 HONGKONG/HKDOLLAR (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|NAT|SI|UKI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090508e558000gf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
