\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Hong Kong - April 23\b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2009\par 02:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, April 23 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in Hong Kong newspapers on Thursday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.\par \par \uc2 APPLE DAILY -- \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has changed to a positive tone for China's GDP growth this year as it revised up the growth prediction to 8.3 percent from six percent for 2009.\par \par \uc2 MING PAO DAILY NEWS -- \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  has revised up its previous target price estimate on CITIC Pacific, but it is still lower than the current share price. The group believes the Chinese firm would continue to offload its non-core assets.\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG ECONOMIC TIMES -- Chief Secretary Henry Tang will lead a Hong Kong business delegation today to begin a tour in Guangdong province to visit nine cities and towns to promote tighter economic ties.\par \par \uc2 SING TAO DAILY -- Sources say PCCW will not seek to extend the privatisation plan's for a later long stop date, meaning today the privatisation proposal will terminate. The company was also said to be declaring a high dividend today about HK$1.30 per share.\par \par \uc2 SOUTH CHINA MORNING POST -- A report from the Director of Audit has criticised spending at the Equal Opportunities Commission ranging from a HK$15,200 dinner in Beijing to the payment of a HK$320 speeding ticket.\par \par \uc2 -- PCCW chairman Richard Li Tzar-kai and \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  are considering walking away from their six-month campaign to buy out minority shareholders of the telecoms giant following a ruling yesterday by the Court of Appeal that effectively blocked the controversial HK$15.93 billion deal.\par \par \uc2 THE STANDARD -- The International Monetary Fund says many loans on the balance sheets of institutions are eroding in value. So it is not only subprime securities - which first triggered the financial crisis - that are in trouble.\par \par \uc2 -- AsiaWorld-Expo has called on the government to press ahead with the second phase of its expansion to meet the anticipated increase in bookings upon the completion of the Hong Kong- Zhuhai-Macau Bridge and Hong Kong-Shenzhen Airport Link near its site in Chek Lap Kok.\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG ECONOMIC JOURNAL -- Individual shareholders of PCCW said they believe chairman Richard Li won't take the case to Court of Final Appeal. But he is yet to make a decision following Wednesday's court decision to overturn the previous ruling that sustained the company's privatisation deal.\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG DAILY NEWS -- The Hang Seng Index closed at its intra-day low yesterday at 14,878, losing 407 points or 2.7 percent. Analysts say the bear market bounce previously was of the weaker type because it was not supported by gains in blue-chip prices.\par \par \uc2 SING PAO -- \uc2 Husky Oil\uc2  in Canada, partially-owned by Hong Kong billionnaire Li Ka-shing, is planning to spin off it Southeast Asian assets for separate public listing in Asia. Assets will be injected under the plan when market conditions improve.\par \par \uc2 WEN WEI PO -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars as the exchange under peg went up to the strong end of the threshold against the greenback. Aggregated balance in the Hong Kong banking system will rise to over HK$170.4 billion today.\par \par \uc2 TA KUNG PAO -- Hong Kong-listed TCL Communication Technology Holdings has issued a profit warning for its first quarter performance. Its core \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  would dip into the red as sales volume drops. For Chinese newspapers, see..............