\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: HK Exchange Fund Has Record HK$81.0B Net Invest Loss\b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2009\par 13:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Yam on outlook for financial markets, and comments from analysts on fund's asset allocation; breakdown of exchange fund performance.)\par \par \uc2 By Chester Yung\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Sliding stock markets pushed Hong Kong's Exchange Fund to a record net investment loss of HK$81.0 billion (US$10.4 billion) in 2008, and Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said it is difficult to predict when a turnaround can start.\par \par \uc2 A loss had been widely expected in the Exchange Fund, which has a target allocation of 77% bonds and 23% local and foreign stocks, and analysts said the loss announced Tuesday was manageable although it could make a case for the HKMA to show more flexibility in the Exchange Fund's asset allocations.\par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund's assets totaled HK$1.558 trillion on Dec. 31, up from HK$1.414 trillion a year earlier. The increase came about as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the last four months of 2008, adding to its aggregate balance.\par \par \uc2 Yam told a news conference the Exchange Fund faces more difficult times in 2009, with a rebound tough to call.\par \par \uc2 "The global financial system remains unsettled," Yam said.\par \par \uc2 "Whether monetary easing and large fiscal stimulus packages in major economies will be effective in restoring investor confidence remain to be seen," Yam said. "Financial markets are under immense stress and we expect volatile conditions to continue for some time."\par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund's net investment loss, its first since 2001, compared with a net gain of HK$132.2 billion in 2007. The Exchange Fund had a negative investment return last year for the first time, of 5.6%. In 2007, its return on investment was 11.8%.\par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund lost HK$77.9 billion on Hong Kong stocks last year, when the benchmark Hang Seng Index slid 48%, and HK$73.2 billion on foreign stocks. It had a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  loss of HK$12.4 billion, mainly due to the depreciation of the U.S. dollar against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 A gain of HK$88.6 billion from bonds offset some of the losses.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that while the loss was widely expected, the Exchange Fund might do better with more flexibility in its asset allocation so it could respond better to market conditions.\par \par \uc2 "For example, the HKMA could allocate more assets from overseas stocks to bonds when bonds are rising and review the allocation frequently," said Frances Cheung, fixed income strategist of Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 DBS senior investment strategist Daniel Chan also said it would be good to review the asset allocations, but he said the loss was not alarming.\par \par \uc2 "With over HK$1.5 trillion of fund total assets, we are still able to afford the temporary losses," Chan said.\par \par \uc2 -By Chester Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; chester.yung@dowjones.com [ 21-01-09 1209GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090121e51l000in\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
