\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Yam says inflow of funds to HK dollar welcome\b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2009\par 09:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, details)\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 15 (Reuters) - Recent fund flows into the Hong Kong dollar, which have pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the top of its trading band, are causing a quantitative easing of monetary conditions, Hong Kong's chief central banker said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The inflow of funds to the Hong Kong dollar that we have been seeing is a welcome development," Joseph Yam, Chief Executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a weekly column on the HKMA's Website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.info.gov.hk/hkma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.info.gov.hk/hkma}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Although we do not practise discretionary monetary management, quantitative easing is made possible by the substantial inflow of money that we have been seeing since the global financial crisis turned sharply worse with the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong does not have control over monetary policy because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg with the U.S. dollar forces it to track U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Yam said monetary easing was appropriate given that Hong Kong is in recession and inflation is easing. Short-term interbank rates have fallen to near zero as a result of the HKMA injecting massive funds into the market to stem appreciation of the Hong Kong dollar and keep it within its pegged trading band with the U.S. dollar, he said.\par \par \uc2 Yam reiterated a warning earlier this month that fund flows could reverse but said that should be an orderly process. The HKMA has U.S. dollars it can sell back into the market and make a profit, unlike authorities elsewhere that have increased liquidity by buying up domestic financial assets, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Our position contrasts favourably with that of jurisdictions that have injected money into the banking system through buying domestic financial assets," Yam said. "Domestic circumstances may not be conducive to selling these assets back to the market when it becomes necessary to exit from quantitative easing."\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar <HKD=> has been trading at or close to the top of its fixed trading band with the U.S. dollar since the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse in mid-September as risk-averse investors have unwound carry trades and as some have seen Hong Kong, with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  link to the U.S. dollar, as a safe haven to park funds.\par \par \uc2 Heavy fund inflows have forced the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and sell massive amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars for U.S. dollars to keep Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its trading band. (Reporting by Susan Fenton; Editing by Nick Macfie)\par \par \uc2 HONGKONG-HKMA/YAM (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|Z|M|D|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090115e51f000nb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
