\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Stocks May Not Keep Propping Hong Kong Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rosalind Mathieson   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2008\par 01:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar has been an outperformer amid the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-off in recent months, but its reliance on inflows to the stock market may yet leave it vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 The flow of money in and out of Hong Kong is a large driver of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , within the peg arrangement against the U.S. dollar, of course.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the Hong Kong dollar has pushed a fair few times against the upper bound of its peg range, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Part of that has been due to a rise in domestic interbank rates and the unwinding of carry trades into the U.S. dollar, and part of it has been due to resilience in the Hong Kong stock market.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the Hang Seng Index has risen 8.3% so far in December, gaining three of the past four trading days, though it remains down 45.9% year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 But Monday's action suggests the gains may prove vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 The HSI surged 8.7% on Monday in heavy volume to its highest close in almost two months, over the key 15,000 level, led by gains in China-based companies.\par \par \uc2 Part of that was due to hopes for further efforts by Beijing to support the mainland economy (and thus Hong Kong by association, given the dual listing of many companies and the reliance of some Hong Kong companies on income from Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 This week brings the annual Central Economic Work Conference in China, too, a key summit of leaders which some think could yield fresh measures to stimulate the economy - or at least flesh out the details of the fiscal plans already announced.\par \par \uc2 Another key driver of Monday's rally was hopes the long-delayed program aimed at allowing mainland Chinese residents to directly invest in Hong Kong's stock market, known as the "through train," would finally get the go-ahead; Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said the program at least hadn't been scrapped.\par \par \uc2 That prompted a flurry of money into the Hong Kong stock market on hopes among investors generally that mainland Chinese would soon be pumping shedloads of cash in.\par \par \uc2 But we've seen previous episodes where hopes have been raised for its start, only for them to later be dashed and for cash to move back out of Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The latest anticipation is likely to prove inflated.\par \par \uc2 Tsang himself noted the ongoing difficulties in getting the program up and running, none the least on the logistical side.\par \par \uc2 And with the large interest rate cuts, liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fiscal stimulus measures underway in China, the central government is not about to torpedo the Shanghai stock market by allowing Chinese to exit en masse and invest in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Managing Director for China equities, Jing Ulrich, believes officials will turn their focus in the new year to supporting stocks and encouraging property purchases; "with the economy expected to further weaken in the near-term, the risk of rising unemployment and income insecurity could create stumbling blocks for a sustained recovery," she says.\par \par \uc2 Her counterpart at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , economist Frank Gong, has also warned of the risk of social unrest after the Chinese new year period, especially if many migrant workers fail to find jobs in February.\par \par \uc2 That's hardly a time to be letting money leave in large scale from the stock market, which has risen 10.6% over the past four trading days but remains 60% lower year-to-date and tends to be volatile..\par \par \uc2 As well, the expectations surrounding the Central Economic Work Conference may be overblown; we could see a steady drip-feed of commentary from key officials talking about the need to support the economy, but little in the way of fresh specifics.\par \par \uc2 That could dent sentiment on the China-linked plays listed in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 And that in turn could put a lid on the Hong Kong dollar in the near term.\par \par \uc2 (Rosalind Mathieson is Asia-Pacific Managing Editor for Market News at Dow Jones Newswires. She has 14 years of experience as a journalist and editor, having worked for news organizations in Singapore and Australia. She can be contacted on 65-64154-141 or by email: rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 09-12-08 0051GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 5008 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081209e4c90000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
