\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Safety rather than speculation in HK dollar strength\b0\par\par\uc2 By Susan Fenton   \par \uc2 969 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2008\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 25 (Reuters) - A rush of cash into safe-haven markets has flooded Hong Kong's banks with funds and forced the Hong Kong dollar to the top of its trading band, belying market expectations for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to eventually weaken.\par \par \uc2 Demand for Hong Kong dollars has surged as volatile credit markets have prompted a heavy unwinding of carry trades while Hong Kong's robust banking system and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar have made the territory a safer place to park funds.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly hit the top of its pegged trading band with the U.S. dollar at 7.75 <HKD=>, and the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has injected $7.1 billion into the market in the past month to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its band.\par \par \uc2 Most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts and economists see little danger to the Hong Kong dollar peg. There is also little fundamental support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from an economy in recession.\par \par \uc2 "When the liquidity squeeze normalises, market trades will go back to fundamentals," said Emmanuel Ng, a strategist at \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  in Singapore. "Demand for Hong Kong dollars will ease and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will head back to the centre of its trading band."\par \par \uc2 As of now, even the forward markets contradict that outlook. As investors and banks use swaps to pile into Hong Kong, the forward market <HKDF=> <0#HKDF=> is pricing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 7.73 to the U.S. dollar within 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Such is the nature of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Board that the HKMA has to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and mopping up the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to preserve the peg. The surplus cash in the banking system is around HK$84 billion, a record level compared with levels of just HK$5 billion in August.\par \par \uc2 Defending the peg has become a challenge for the HKMA, not to mention the inflationary pressures that could ensue.\par \par \uc2 Yet, the Hong Kong authorities are in the enviable position of fighting an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and influx of cash, at a time when most central banks are trying to staunch capital flight.\par \par \uc2 Bank-to-bank lending rates <HIBOR=R> are at 5-month lows, and even below U.S. dollar rates.\par \par \uc2 "They have a problem which a lot of countries would love to have at the moment -- the rich man's dilemma," said an analyst in Singapore, who declined to be named.\par \par \uc2 YUAN PLAY\par \par \uc2 Unlike in 2007, when speculators pushed the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the top of its trading limit, this time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be driven purely by a flight to safety. Nor is it considered undervalued for it to become a speculative bet.\par \par \uc2 Rather, real demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during a market panic -- heightened by banks' requirement for extra liquidity amid tight credit -- masks a shift in longer-term sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "The market no longer expects the Hong Kong dollar to appreciate (long term). It expects it to depreciate. That's a structural change," said Joe Lo, senior economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is often seen as a proxy for the Chinese yuan, which is not fully convertible, and the shift in sentiment coincides with a reversal in expectations for yuan appreciation.\par \par \uc2 A rising yuan had been viewed as a one-way bet since the yuan was revalued in July 2005 but is now seen to be putting too much pressure on exporters struggling with weakening global demand.\par \par \uc2 Still, in the short term, the Hong Kong dollar is likely to remain strong as investors continue to seek safe havens.\par \par \uc2 "Against the current backdrop, investors are asking, not "Where can I make money?" but "Where can I avoid losing too much money?" said Sebastien Barbe, senior economist at Calyon in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 HKMA Chief Executive Joseph Yam said last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not seen any anomalies in the market and was ready to continue injecting liquidity to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its band.\par \par \uc2 "The (recent) strong demand for Hong Kong dollars was probably due to repatriation of funds and unwinding of interest carry trades as global financial jitters led market participants to deleverage and reduce their exposure to risk," Yam said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's decision last month to guarantee all customer bank deposits in the territory, and its peg to the U.S. dollar while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain volatile, have reinforced Hong Kong's attraction as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 As the collapse of U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in mid-September sparked panic in global financial markets, Hong Kong's banking system drew net inflows of HK$90 billion that month -- the largest amount in 11 months, according to \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong banks have had limited exposure to U.S. subprime risk and their aggregate classified-loan ratio is at less than 1 percent, down from more than 10 percent after the Asian financial crisis a decade ago, according to the HKMA.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect global investors to remain risk averse for now but say confidence could improve in a few months.\par \par \uc2 If credit markets stabilise, carry trades could resume, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the weak side of its trading band between 7.80 and 7.85 where it was entrenched before Lehman's collapse.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85.\par \par \uc2 And if the yuan keeps slowing meanwhile, the Hong Kong dollar could head to the bottom of its band at 7.85, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 That would be the time to worry about the peg. (US$=HK$7.8) (Editing by Vidya Ranganathan and Kazunori Takada)\par \par \uc2 HONGKONG/HKDOLLAR (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081128e4bp000jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
