\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: HKMA Exchange Fund Jan-Sep Invest Losses HK$83.3B\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2008\par 08:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more Exchange Fund data, Yam's comment on 4Q and the strengthening of the Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar.)\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong Exchange Fund's investment losses more than doubled in the third quarter to HK$48.3 billion when compared with the previous quarter as global stock markets fell, Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The latest loss compared with a HK$20.4 billion loss in the second quarter and brought the Exchange Fund's losses for the nine months ended Sept. 30 to HK$83.3 billion, Yam told lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Yam told lawmakers the fund will incur losses in the fourth quarter due to a "very bad" investment environment in October and November. He said the full-year loss will exceed HK$83.3 billion, but didn't provide a specific estimate.\par \par \uc2 The investment losses this year come after the Exchange Fund posted record earnings of HK$142.2 billion in 2007, up from HK$103.8 billion in 2006.\par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund's loss on its Hong Kong stock investments during January-September totaled HK$56.9 billion, as the benchmark Hang Seng Index fell 35% during the period.\par \par \uc2 It had a loss of HK$43.2 billion on overseas stock investments and a loss of HK$13.8 billion on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the nine-month period, but a HK$30.6 billion gain from bonds over the same period pared some losses.\par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund, made up of a variety of assets, including stocks and bonds, amounted to HK$1.426 trillion as of Sept. 30 and is used by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar and ensure financial stability in the territory.\par \par \uc2 Yam attributed the recent strengthening of the Hong Kong dollar to the unwinding of long U.S. dollar positions due to growing risk aversion among market participants and possible repatriation of funds by domestic corporations.\par \par \uc2 Strong demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar in recent weeks prompted the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more than a dozen times this week to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar. Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record HK$18.2 billion Thursday over seven \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggregate balance to HK$84.27 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Chester Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; chester.yung@dowjones.com [ 21-11-08 0730GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081121e4bl0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
