\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Asian Shares Mixed; Japan Falls But HK Up\b0\par\par\uc2 1,085 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2008\par 07:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates and adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets were mixed Wednesday in low trading volume despite a rise in U.S. stocks, with tech stocks pressured by worries about the outlook for consumer spending around the globe.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 ended with a 0.7% decline 2.5% after falling 2.3% Tuesday, with Korea's Kospi Composite finishing down 1.9% after a 3.9% drop Tuesday; Australia's S&P/ASX 200 slipped 0.7%, following a 3.6% fall Tuesday, with the lifting of a ban there on short-selling of most non-financial stocks. But there was some bounceback in Chinese stocks with Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index adding 1.6% after a 4.5% drop Tuesday, taking its lead from the Shanghai Composite Index, which had jumped 5.6% in a late-day rise.\par \par \uc2 Even so, sentiment on the Chinese economy remained wobbly at best, despite the recent news of a large-sized fiscal stimulus package.\par \par \uc2 The market mood generally was cautious in Asia, and trade was volatile.\par \par \uc2 "Investor confidence has been decimated and all of us are looking for a catalyst, but it continues to be a struggle for many companies worldwide," said Barry Lindsay, director of research at First NZ Capital.\par \par \uc2 "People have got it into their heads that the downturn is going to be deep, protracted and ugly really. They have got no confidence about earnings and can't seem to price shares."\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures were down 0.5% to 1% in screen trade, with European spreadbetting firms calling stocks there to open slightly lower; overnight, the Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 1.8% in a late-day rally, but the Nasdaq Composite only managed to end 0.1% higher.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, concerns over the extent of the global economic slowdown continue to weigh across the board for stocks - something that will be played out with falling commodity prices and continued pressure on banks," said CMC Markets dealer Matt Buckland.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks failed to get much help from \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2 's 14% rise in the U.S. after the computer maker forecast fiscal 2009 profit ahead of the average Wall Street estimate, showing that its diversified business model would help it avoid the most painful effects of a slowdown in computer sales.\par \par \uc2 H-P's approach seemed company-specific, analysts said; also, the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index fell 2% Tuesday amid broad concerns about the demand outlook in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Markets were awaiting the release later Wednesday of U.S. CPI data and the latest Federal Open Market Committee meeting minutes.\par \par \uc2 They were also watching for news on the U.S. auto sector with the Senate potentially gearing up to vote on a rescue package for the industry, amid warnings from several large carmakers that they might run out of cash; some lawmakers were critical of the idea of bailing out auto firms.\par \par \uc2 "The troubled U.S. automotive industry poses a large economic and potentially systemic risk, so a lack of a rescue plan is bad news for risky assets in the near term," said analysts at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lower trading volume was exacerbating volatility; "on the plus side, selling seems to be drying up, but it's just not clear where the positive catalysts are going to come from for a sustainable rally," said a trader a local brokerage in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, financial shares tumbled on \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2 's poor results for the six months ended September; Mitsubishi sank 6.4% while \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group\uc2  fell 7.5%.\par \par \uc2 Exporter stocks were hurt by a rising yen with \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  down 5.5% and TDK off 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, the main index briefly hit its lowest level since May 2004; "share prices would indicate there's some shorting out there," said Patrick Crabb, senior sales trader at \uc2 Goldman Sachs JBWere\uc2  in Melbourne, with falls in large-cap stocks like BHP, Woolworths, Woodside and Brambles, though some banking shares were higher.\par \par \uc2 OZ Minerals\uc2  fell 14% after it warned falling metals prices would hurt profits and said volatility made it impossible to give meaningful 2008 profit guidance; BHP Billiton fell 4.1% with \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  down 3.1%.\par \par \uc2 Korean oil refiners fell on concerns about their earnings outlook, given shrinking margins and a weakening global demand; SK Energy ended down 5% while S-Oil fell 3%.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's main index ended 0.5% lower with tech stocks weak; New Zealand's NZX-50 ended down 0.3% though Philippine shares gained 1.9%; Singapore's Straits Times Index was recently down 0.9% with Indonesian shares down 1.8%, but India's Sensex up 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar was at Y96.65 from Y97.15 earlier, while the euro was down at Y122.02, from Y122.73.\par \par \uc2 The euro was around $1.2623, down a little from late in New York.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were on the decline: The U.S. dollar was up at KRW1,451 against the Korean won while the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to be selling dollars to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  though had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times to inject \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar liquidity, in a bid to support the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The cost of protection against default on Asian debt continued to widen, with the Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade credit default swaps index quoted at 390 to 430 basis points, from 390 basis points this time Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $737.75 a troy ounce, up 45 cents from New York levels; LME metals were lower with three-month copper down $59 at $3,691 a metric ton and aluminum down $10 at $1,908.\par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude was up 13 cents at $54.52 a barrel on Globex with investors awaiting the release of U.S. weekly energy inventory data later Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 19-11-08 0649GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081119e4bj0006g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
