\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOJ, RBA, MAS Inject Cash; Officials Soothe Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 10:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds further injections by BOJ, MAS)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Central banks and other officials around Asia leapt into action Tuesday with either cash or soothing words to reassure the short-term borrowing and othermarkets after the U.S. House of Representatives voted down a key US$700-billion U.S. financial sector bailout package.\par \par \uc2 That decision sent the Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbling 7% overnight, taking Asian bourses and manyof the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with it Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  injected Y3.0trillion into the short-term money market in two batchesto tackle an overnight call rate that was as high as 0.80%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target rate is 0.50%.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has now injected Y21.1 trillion into the money market over the 10 consecutive business days since U.S. investment bank \uc2 Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc\uc2 . filed for bankruptcy protection.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  made a much smaller net injection of A$75 million in its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, that will keep the level of commercial banks' exchange settlement accounts at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around A$10.7 billion, the highest since the U.S. subprime-inspired market turmoil began making the headlines in mid-2007.\par \par \uc2 Money market rates in Australia were relatively steady, with the three-month bank bill swap rate fixed Tuesday at 7.32%, unchanged from Monday. The one-month rate is down 2 basis points at 7.38%. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Tuesday injected S$1.3 billion Tuesday to tackle an overnight rate of over 1.8%. The action pushed the rate down to around 1.375% and followed an injection of an estimated S$2.5 billion Monday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, others used verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm the local markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Joseph Yam said he expected the interbank funding market and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to be tense after the bailout was rejected but vowed to provide liquidity to the market and to banks if necessary.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman at the Reserve \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2  said it "has the systems in place" to cope with the current global market shakeout, although that didn't stop some analysts from speculating that the RBNZ may have to continue with aggressive rate cuts, and possibly even an emergency easing, to prevent the country from sliding into a steep recession.\par \par \uc2 And in South Korea, the Bank of Korea said it had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the swap market for a week to calm the markets and would continue to do so until the market stabilizes.\par \par \uc2 Regulators and government leaders also felt the need to weigh in, expressing dismay over the failed package and hope that there would be a rethink.\par \par \uc2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan said he was "disappointed" that the U.S. package failed to pass, but nevertheless emphasized the differences between the struggling U.S. financial system and Australia's system.\par \par \uc2 "Our regulators...are in constant contact with all of our deposit-taking institutions," Swan said. "The advice that we are receiving is that (the system) is in good shape."\par \par \uc2 Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd Tuesday urged U.S. lawmakers to reconsider the package and said friends and allies of the U.S. would push the U.S. Congress to pass the package, or similar measures later this week.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang was more positive, saying he believed in the U.S. Congress's resolve to approve the plan, adding the rejection would severely damage global economies.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, eight central banks around the world, including the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , announced they were more than doubling the dollar-swap facility with the Federal Reserve to US$620 billion from US$290 billion to unclog markets scrambling to meet dollar-funding needs ahead of the quarter-end.\par \par \uc2 Japan doubled the size its swap facility with the Fed to US$120 billion while Australia tripled its facility to US$30 billion.\par \par \uc2 "A dollar-liquidity problem has been intensifying in Japan, and we need to make every effort to ease" the issue, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor Masaaki Shirakawa at a press conference overnight.\par \par \uc2 The RBA said Tuesday that it would announce in the next few days another U.S. dollar repurchase agreement \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject U.S. dollars into the financial system as part of the swap line expansion.\par \par \uc2 -By Karen Lane, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4043; karen.lane@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Iain McDonald and Enda Curran in Sydney, Jin-Young Yook in Seoul, Ben Garvey in Tokyo and Jackie Cheung in Hong Kong contributed to this story.) [ 30-09-08 0858GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080930e49u000i8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
