\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: HKMA Injects Funds In Mkt; More To Come-Analysts\b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2008\par 10:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds analysts' comments on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .) \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 HONG KONG\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Dow Jones)--The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's injection of HK$1.556 billion into the interbank market Thursday is unlikely to be the last such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the current global liquidity crunch, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "Clearly, what they're doing is what everybody is doing, to provide as much liquidity as they can to the market, as the market is in such an irrational state," said \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  analyst Claudio Piron.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank offered rates retreated from earlier highs after the HKMA announced the liquidity injection in the early afternoon. Thursday's rise was driven by strong demand for liquidity in the current credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 The one-month Hibor was last at 4.03%, down from a high of around 4.80% earlier in the session, but still up sharply from 2.22% late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The three-month Hibor was last at 2.75%, retreating from a high of 3.0% earlier in the session, but up from 2.16% late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said it was abnormal for short-dated Hibors to be higher than the long-dated counterpart.\par \par \uc2 "This implies that liquidity is extremely tight due to the credit concerns across the global markets," said Frances Cheung, fixed-income strategist at Standard Chartered Bank. "The HKMA's move is simply to ease the tight liquidity."\par \par \uc2 Cheung said a reasonable level for the one-month Hibor would be around 3%.\par \par \uc2 At 0755 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at HK$7.7821, down from HK$7.7838 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The latest injection increases Hong Kong's aggregate balance, a measure of interbank liquidity, to HK$6.40 billion from HK$4.73 billion.\par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the Hong Kong dollar is pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar but is allowed to trade between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85. When the Hong Kong dollar reaches the limits of its trading band, the HKMA can be expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The HKMA last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Oct. 31, when it bought U.S. dollars to halt the Hong Kong dollar's rise.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the Hong Kong dollar had hovered near the top end of its trading band to the U.S. dollar before the HKMA stepped in to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Amy Or and Chester Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; amy.or@dowjones.com [ 18-09-08 0829GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080918e49i000fr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
