\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: HK Fincl Secy: To Increase Reserves To Defend Peg   \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2007\par 11:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from Tsang, background of Exchange Fund.)   \par \par \uc2 By Jackie Cheung   \par \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Monday the government plans to gradually increase its fiscal reserves to maintain monetary and fiscal stability, giving it greater ability to defend against any attacks on the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Tsang told legislators he expects the government's reserves will gradually increase to HK$587.9 billion (US$75.4 billion) in the fiscal year ended 2012, representing 30.2% of GDP, up from the current fiscal year's HK$394.7 billion, which accounts for 25.7% of GDP.   \par \par \uc2 "We have seen the speculation last week, and we expect the market to remain volatile, so we want to increase our reserves as much as possible to defend our peg," Tsang told legislators. "We shall maintain the fiscal reserves to the extent necessary to meet our needs."   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar and allows it to trade within a band of HK$7.75-HK$7.85 to the U.S. unit.   \par \par \uc2 Last week, the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets five times, selling a total of HK$7.828 billion for U.S. dollars, as the local dollar reached the upper limit of its trading band on inflows of funds into \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's red-hot stock market. The HKMA had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice the previous week with a smaller amount.   \par \par \uc2 The government's fiscal reserves are transferred to the Exchange Fund, which is under the management of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  and can be used to back the issuance of Hong Kong banknotes.   \par \par \uc2 According to the HKMA, the total assets of the Exchange Fund amounted to HK$1.31 trillion at the end of September, up from HK$1.14 trillion a year earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Tsang said inflation - driven by higher food prices, a weaker U.S. dollar and a vibrant stock market - will be one of his major concerns for coming years.   \par \par \uc2 He expects the composite consumer price index may rise between 1.8% and 2% this year, though he said the level isn't particularly high taking into account the better-than-expected 6.1% growth in first-half gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; jackie.cheung@dowjones.com [ 05-11-07 1057GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071105e3b5000mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
