\par\par \b \uc2 HK Fincl Secy:To Increase Fiscal Reserves To HK$587.9B By 2012   \b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2007\par 05:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang said Monday the government plans to gradually increase its fiscal reserves to maintain monetary and fiscal stability, following recent speculative activity targeting the U.S. dollar peg.   \par \par \uc2 Tsang told legislators the government will increase its reserves to HK$587.9 billion in the fiscal year ended 2012, representing 30.2% of GDP, up from HK$394.7 billion in the current fiscal year, which accounts for 25.7% of GDP.   \par \par \uc2 "We have seen the speculation last week, and we expect the market to remain volatile, so we want to increase our reserves as much as possible," Tsang told legislators.   \par \par \uc2 The local unit rose sharply against the U.S. dollar last week as investors poured money into Hong Kong's red-hot stock market.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets five times last week, selling a total of HK$7.828 billion to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; jackie.cheung@dowjones.com [ 05-11-07 0434GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071105e3b500048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
