\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong says  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  peg works, no plans to change   \b0\par\par\uc2 202 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2007\par 09:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Nov 1 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong government has no plans to re-peg the Hong Kong dollar at a different level or widen its trading band against the U.S. dollar, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market five times on Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar repeatedly hit the top of its trading band at 7.75 to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a weekly column on Thursday that the linked exchange rate system was working well.   \par \par \uc2 "The government has also reiterated that there is no plan or intention to change the system in any way (such as re-pegging at a different level or widening the convertibility zone)," Yam wrote on the HKMA's Web site.   \par \par \uc2 The convertibility zone refers to the Hong Kong dollar's trading band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar but is allowed to trade between 7.75 and 7.85.   \par \par \uc2 HONGKONG-ECONOMY/HKMA (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071101e3b100125\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
