\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Chief Joseph Yam To Retire In 2009 - Source   \b0\par\par\uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2007\par 12:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will retire from his post in 2009, a government official familiar with the situation said Sunday.   \par \par \uc2 The government official told Dow Jones Newswires it's too soon for a decision to be made on a replacement for Yam and the change in leadership at the HKMA won't affect the government's commitment to maintain the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 HKMA spokesman Thomas Chan declined to comment, but said Yam will be 60 next year, the typical retirement age for civil servants in Hong Kong.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; jackie.cheung@dowjones.com [ 28-10-07 1127GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with background on recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , quotes from commercial bank official, research report)   \par \par \uc2 By Jackie Cheung   \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong monetary chief Joseph Yam, for years a staunch supporter of the peg linking Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, will retire in 2009, a government official said Sunday.   \par \par \uc2 The news of Yam's planned departure from the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes at a time when \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar has been rising, prompting the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week to keep the dollar in its trading band of 7.85-7.75 to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 That recent activity in the market has raised some questions among analysts about whether the peg might someday be abandoned or modified, and the news on Yam could add fuel to that debate.   \par \par \uc2 The government official who confirmed Yam's retirement plans to Dow Jones Newswires said that the change in leadership at the HKMA would not affect the government's commitment to the peg.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong leaders have repeatedly referred to the peg as a sacred cornerstone of the territory's monetary policy that will be maintained.   \par \par \uc2 The government official, who spoke on condition he not be identified, said it was too soon to announce a replacement for Yam, who has held the top post at the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1993, when Hong Kong was still a British colony.   \par \par \uc2 Newspaper speculation on a successor has centered around Norman Chan, formerly the deputy chief executive of the HKMA and now the office director for Hong Kong's government leader, Chief Executive Donald Tsang.   \par \par \uc2 HKMA spokesman Thomas Chan declined comment on whether Yam would be leaving. But Chan said that next year Yam will be 60, the typical retirement age for civil servants in Hong Kong.   \par \par \uc2 The HKMA bought HK$775 million in U.S. dollars on Tuesday and Friday - its first \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since May 2005 - to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar away from the top end of its trading band.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar has been rising as investors pour money into the local stock market, which closed at a fresh record above 30,000 points Friday. At the same time, the U.S. dollar has been sinking against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 As has happened in the past when \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and defend the peg, questions have been raised about whether the peg can stand.   \par \par \uc2 The debate pits so-called "peggers," who believe Hong Kong will maintain the peg, against "floaters," who believe the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  someday will be delinked from the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 A top trader at Hang Seng Bank, senior manager Dennis Wong, told Dow Jones Newswires Sunday evening that the HKMA might have to carry out more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term to keep the peg in place, but he doesn't think Yam's departure signals any changes in policy.   \par \par \uc2 "The Hong Kong dollar looks set to test the band again no matter what, and the HKMA will have to step in several more times to defend the peg next week," Wong said.   \par \par \uc2 "But there aren't any grounds for the exchange rate system to be changed because of the change in leadership. The government has reiterated its commitment in the linked exchange rate system," Wong said.   \par \par \uc2 But in a research note Friday, before news of Yam's departure appeared in Hong Kong newspapers, ING head of research and chief economist Tim Condon said he believes the HKMA would widen the local unit's trading band, allowing it to rise against the U.S. dollar and better absorb growing demand for Hong Kong equities.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; Jackie.cheung@dowjones.com. [ 28-10-07 1341GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071028e3as0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
