\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong intervenes \highlight\uc2  to defend its  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  peg   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Susan Fenton and Christina Lo   \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2007\par 06:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, details)   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oct 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to curb strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and underline its commitment to the linked exchange rate, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its upper limit for the second time this week.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) sold HK$775 million (US$100 million) for U.S. dollars after the most actively traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia after the yen hit 7.75 <HKD=> per U.S. dollar, partly fuelled by a stock market <.HSI> rally to a record high.   \par \par \uc2 "The government is fully committed to the maintenance of the linked exchange rate system," a spokesman for the HKMA said.   \par \par \uc2 Funds flowing into Hong Kong, plus quickening inflation as Chinese import costs rise, have fuelled speculation the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) will have to drop its two-decade peg against the U.S. dollar, or at least widen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band.   \par \par \uc2 ING Financial Markets said in a research note that it expected Hong Kong to widen the band in time, possibly only at the upper limit. But for now economists said abandoning the peg was unlikely.   \par \par \uc2 "We see no change. The HKMA's actions today agree with the principles of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board," said Ben Simpfendorfer, an economist at  \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of interest at the moment in the Hong Kong dollar in particular because of a buoyant equity market. We think the peg is credible and it makes no sense to change it."   \par \par \uc2 Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the Hong Kong dollar can trade in a range of 7.75-7.85 per dollar, around a peg of 7.8. The HKMA is committed to buy U.S. dollars for Hong Kong dollars if the spot rate hits 7.75.   \par \par \uc2 Real time information available on Reuters page <HKMAOOC> showed the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0425 GMT. By 0649 GMT the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar had pulled back to 7.7505/06 to the U.S. dollar, Reuters data showed.   \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the HKMA also sold HK$775 million for U.S. dollars after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 7.75. The upper limit of the trading band was only set in 2005. Previously the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pegged at 7.8 per dollar with a lower limit of 7.85.   \par \par \uc2 CREDIT SQUEEZE   \par \par \uc2 Although the stock market has been rallying all year, the Hong Kong dollar was held in check earlier in 2007 by an interest rate trade in the interbank market.   \par \par \uc2 The trade was based on the fact that Hong Kong interbank interest rates were lower than corresponding rates in the United States.   \par \par \uc2 That meant investors could profit by selling Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars to exploit the higher U.S. interbank rates. The interest rate advantage has now been lost, so fewer Hong Kong dollars are flowing out of the territory.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank rates subsequently rose to six-year highs amid tight demand for Hong Kong dollars to fund investment in recent new stock market listings and as a global credit squeeze made lender more cautious.   \par \par \uc2 While inflation has been quickening this year, partly because of the rising cost of Chinese imports as the yuan <CNY=CFXS> steadily increases in value, economists say it remains relatively moderate at less than 3 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "At a time when Hong Kong's domestic CPI inflation trend is heading up gradually, it is perhaps not surprising that there has been renewed market concerns on the sustainability of the Hong Kong dollar peg regime,"  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's head of China research, Frank Gong, wrote in a research note.   \par \par \uc2 But he added: "We believe the probability of any significant near-term change to the Hong Kong dollar peg regime is very low." (Additional reporting by Donny Kwok) ($1=HK$7.750)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS/HKMA (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071026e3aq0009c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
