\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA sells HK$775 million of HKD to curb strength   \b0\par\par\uc2 234 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2007\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Oct 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday afternoon as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <HKD=D2> hovered just a whisker below its upper trading-band limit of 7.75 to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  data <HKMAOOC> signs that it sold HK$775 million worth of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in afternoon business to boost supply and curb the unit's strength.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar had eased to 7.7511 per dollar by 0709 GMT. It traded at its upper limit of 7.75 to the U.S. dollar on Tuesday morning for the first time since the trading band was set in May 2005, propped up by persistent fund inflows.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said some players were also speculating that Hong Kong might eventually have to abandon its peg with the U.S. dollar or widen its trading band to let the Hong Kong dollar appreciate more, as a strengthening Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> and a weak U.S. dollar meant the territory was importing inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar but can trade between 7.75 and 7.85.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS/HKMA (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071023e3an000j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
