\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:HKMA Yam: No Need To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If HKD Within Band   \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2007\par 12:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst's comments, latest \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar spot rate.)   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said Monday there is no need for the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar remains within its trading band.   \par \par \uc2 The local unit is pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar and is allowed to trade between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85. It dropped to a 22-year low of HK$7.8251 to the U.S. dollar late Wednesday, but has since rebounded.   \par \par \uc2 In late Asian trade Monday, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.8049, down from HK$7.8105 late Friday. The U.S. unit was fixed at HK$7.8090 earlier Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking at a meeting of the Legislative Council's financial affairs panel, Yam called the Hong Kong dollar's recent movement "healthy," and said it hadn't prompted any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Yam added the HKMA hasn't carried out any market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.   \par \par \uc2 "A certain level of spread between Hong Kong dollar and U.S. dollar interest rates isn't inconsistent with a credible convertibility zone. We don't see the need for market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the relationship between interest rates and the exchange rate for now," Yam said.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar had been rebounding even before Yam made his comments early Monday, and traders said his comments weren't a surprise to the market. They added the Hong Kong dollar remains close to the peg rate, making it unnecessary for the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.   \par \par \uc2 Yam said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's recent volatility was largely the result of large initial public offerings that temporarily boosted demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Demand for the Hong Kong dollar returned to normal after banks refunded cash to those who unsuccessfully subscribed for the placements, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the outlook for the Hong Kong dollar hinges on the mainland's A-share market in the near term. An extended weakness in the mainland bourses may spill over to the Hong Kong market and cause international investors to switch capital out of Hong Kong, they said.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is more concerned about the overheating A-share market for now," said  \uc2 Citic Ka Wah Bank\uc2  chief economist and strategist K.C. Law.   \par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index tumbled 8.3% Monday to close at 3,670.40 points, posting its biggest daily percentage drop since Feb. 27.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung and Amy Or, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832 2335; jackie.cheung@dowjones.com [ 04-06-07 1015GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070604e364000hy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
