\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: HKMA Yam: No Need To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In HK  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2007\par 03:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with comments from HKMA chief executive, Exchange Fund's total asset value.) By Amy Or Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Executive Joseph Yam said he doesn't see the need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell to a 20-month low against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second session in a row Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar at HK$7.80 under the 23-year old Linked Exchange Rate System. In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.8067, up from HK$7.8036 late Wednesday, when it fell below the peg rate for the first time since May 2005.  \par \par \uc2 'So far the Hong Kong dollar hasn't deviated too far from HK$7.80...We didn't see a need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, and we don't see the need to do so now,' Yam said at a briefing.  \par \par \uc2 But he said the HKMA still has the discretion to smooth out volatilities in the Hong Kong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Under reforms to Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate system made in May 2005, the HKMA committed to keeping the Hong Kong dollar within a convertibility band of HK$7.75-HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar, 5 HK cents either side of the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 Yam also said Thursday he is standing by the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA, Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Thursday its Exchange Fund last year posted the highest investment return since 1999, on gains in global equities markets.  \par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund, which holds Hong Kong's foreign reserves, had an investment return of 9.5% in 2006, up from 3.1% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund is used to manage Hong Kong's exchange rate and maintain financial stability. Its total assets stood at HK$1.18 trillion by the end of December.  \par \par \uc2 Unaudited investment income for the Exchange Fund amounted to HK$103.7 billion last year, or HK$93.5 billion after interest and expenses. Its net investment income in the previous year was HK$30.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The investment income was higher than the government's February 2006 estimate of HK$18.2 billion. The Exchange Fund booked an investment income of HK$103.8 billion in 1999 on a rally in stocks that it acquired during the Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 More than half of the fund's gains in 2006, or HK$55.9 billion, came from red-hot local and global equities markets.  \par \par \uc2 Yam said the high investment return in 2006 will likely be repeated this year.  \par \par \uc2 'Economic buoyancy and excess liquidity have led recently to some volatility in asset markets, and this may well increase,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund posted a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain of HK$17 billion in 2006, after an exchange loss of HK$19.5 billion in 2005. About 12% of the Exchange Fund's assets are denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen and euro, with the remainder in U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 It also generated HK$30.5 billion from bonds and other investments, up from HK$4.2 billion in 2005, it said.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong government's share of the Exchange Fund's gains amounted to HK$28.9 billion, up from HK$10 billion the previous year. The administration keeps its accumulated fiscal surpluses from previous years on deposit with the Exchange Fund, meaning it can also benefit from the fund's investment gains. -By Amy Or, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832 2335; amy.or@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070119e31j001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
