\par\par \b \uc2 HK Exchange Fund Investment Return Rises To 9.5% In 2006  \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2007\par 09:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong's Exchange Fund reported Thursday a higher investment return in 2006 on gains in global equities markets.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA's Exchange Fund, which holds Hong Kong's foreign reserves, booked an investment return of 9.5% in 2006, up from 3.1% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund is used to manage Hong Kong's exchange rate and maintain financial stability.  \par \par \uc2 About 12% of the Exchange Fund's assets are denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as yen and euro, with the remainder in U.S. dollars. Its assets stood at US$133.2 billion at the end of December. [ 18-01-07 0850GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Unaudited investment income for the Exchange Fund amounted to HK$103.7 billion last year, or HK$93.5 billion after interest and expenses. Net investment income in the previous year was HK$30.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The figure was higher than the government's February 2006 estimate of HK$18.2 billion.  \par \par \uc2 During the year, the Exchange Fund posted a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain of HK$17 billion. By comparison, an exchange loss of HK$19.5 billion was recorded for 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The Exchange Fund also made a gain of HK$30.5 billion from bonds and other investments, up from HK$4.2 billion in 2005, it said.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong government's share of the Exchange Fund's gains totaled HK$28.9 billion, up from HK$10 billion the previous year. The administration keeps its accumulated fiscal surpluses from previous years on deposit with the Exchange Fund, meaning it can also benefit from the fund's investment gains.  \par \par \uc2 Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also told reporters Thursday that he doesn't see the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell to a 20-month low against the U.S. dollar for a second straight session Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.8067, up from HK$7.8036 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Under reforms to Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rate system made in May 2005, the HKMA committed to keeping the Hong Kong dollar within a convertibility band of HK$7.75-HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar, 5 HK cents either side of the HK$7.80 peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 "So far the HK dollar hasn't deviated too far from HK$7.80...We didn't see a need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, and we don't see the need to do so now," Yam said.  \par \par \uc2 He also said he is standing by the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -By Amy Or, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832 2335; amy.or@dowjones.com [ 18-01-07 0918GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070118e31i000dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
