\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Hong Kong - Jan 13  \b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2006\par 01:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 13 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in Hong Kong newspapers on Friday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG ECONOMIC TIMES  \par \par \uc2 -- The Hong Kong Association of Banks is expected to come to a conclusion this year on the proposed five-day working week plan for local banks. However, not all employees will be affected and branch offices may still open for half a day on Saturdays.  \par \par \uc2 APPLE DAILY  \par \par \uc2 -- People have lined up outside bank branch offices for new banknotes ahead of the Lunar New Year, despite an outcry that producing new notes is costly and not environmentally friendly.  \par \par \uc2 -- Bank of East Asia Ltd. predicts a five percent gain in property values in the second half of the year as interest rates are expected to peak.  \par \par \uc2 MING PAO DAILY NEWS  \par \par \uc2 -- The share price of Lingbao Gold Co. Ltd. shot up 50 percent to as high as HK$5.00 on Thursday in its stock market debut before closing with a 32.9 percent gain at HK$3.33.  \par \par \uc2 SING TAO DAILY  \par \par \uc2 -- Chief Executive Donald Tsang announced yesterday the government will set up a work group to study a five-day working week proposal he put forward for the civil service. The new plan aims for July as an effective start date.  \par \par \uc2 SOUTH CHINA MORNING POST  \par \par \uc2 -- The typical homeowner struggling with negative equity belongs to a middle-income household earning, between HK$20,000 (US$2,564) and HK$50,000 a month, that bought a property smaller than 600 square feet in the New Territories at the height of the 1997 boom.  \par \par \uc2 -- The Hong Kong government is helping the family of a businessman from the city shot dead by robbers at his home in South Africa.  \par \par \uc2 THE STANDARD  \par \par \uc2 -- PCCW Ltd. , the largest fixed-line operator in Hong Kong, will team up with global sports broadcaster ESPN STAR Sports in an effort to wrest one of i-Cable Communications' prize offerings - English Premier League football coverage - from the city's largest pay-TV network. The current contract expires this year.  \par \par \uc2 -- Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. plans to kick off the 2 billion euro (US$2.4 billion) IPO of its Italian 3G mobile business as soon as next week, people familiar with the situation said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG ECONOMIC JOURNAL  \par \par \uc2 -- The market expects the Hong Kong dollar to remain strong as incoming funds are increasing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot up above HK$7.75 against the greenback on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG DAILY NEWS  \par \par \uc2 -- The slowing property market is poised to pick up speed again after the Lunar New Year later this month as many new flats, mostly in the luxury market, are to be offered by developers.  \par \par \uc2 SING PAO  \par \par \uc2 -- Many listed firms have raised funds through share placements in the rising stock market, with ten second-tier players raising money in the past 12 days.  \par \par \uc2 WEN WEI PO  \par \par \uc2 -- The domestic stock market is expected to consolidate now that the Hang Seng Index has recorded an eight-day winning streak. Some analysts believe the index will go back down to near the 15,000-point level after the Lunar New Year in late January.  \par \par \uc2 For mainland China newspapers, see.............. [PRESS/CN]  \par \par \uc2 For Shanghai newspapers, see.......... .[PRESS/CNS]  \par \par \uc2 For Taiwan newspapers, see.[PRESS/TW]  \par \par \uc2 HONGKONG-PRES