\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Hong Kong - May 26  \b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2005\par 02:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 26 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in Hong Kong newspapers on Thursday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG ECONOMIC TIMES  \par \par \uc2 - Chief Secretary and Acting Chief Executive Donald Tsang resigned yesterday to begin his candidacy for the government top post left vacant by Tung Chee-hwa, who resigned two months ago on medical grounds.  \par \par \uc2 New Acting Chief Executive and Financial Secretary Henry Tang has hinted that Tsang, possibly without a rival, will win the election automatically in three weeks.  \par \par \uc2 - Donald Tsang is said to have prepared his election platform for an announcement next week. The labour circle is said to have agreed to gather 40 votes for Tsang and the condition is his support for a minimum wage system.  \par \par \uc2 APPLE DAILY  \par \par \uc2 - Sources say the Hong Kong Mortgage Corp is postponing its retail bond issue in June to the second half year due to the changing interest rate environment.  \par \par \uc2 - Bank of East Asia Chairman David Li says he will rally for votes for Donald Tsang for the chief executive post. However, he has not indicated any involvement with Mr Tsang's campaign.  \par \par \uc2 MING PAO DAILY NEWS  \par \par \uc2 - Former HSBC general manager Raymond Or joined sister bank Hang Seng Bank yesterday as deputy chairman and chief executive. He also introduced a 16-point package aimed at reducing work pressure for the bank's employees, who are known to work long hours.  \par \par \uc2 SING TAO DAILY  \par \par \uc2 - Bank of China group says in its monthly report that there was exaggeration on the urgency for a revaluation of the renminbi and the issue is also politicised.  \par \par \uc2 - Rumours say Rafael Hui would return to the government as chief secretary appointed by Donald Tsang if he is elected as chief executive after his departure five years ago as Secretary for Financial Services.  \par \par \uc2 SOUTH CHINA MORNING POST  \par \par \uc2 - Vincent Cheng returned to familiar territory yesterday after a seven-year hiatus when he reported to work for the first time as chairman of Hong Kong's biggest bank HSBC. Mr Cheng is the first ethnic Chinese to hold the post.  \par \par \uc2 - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown its resolve in maintaining the Hong Kong dollar near the level of $7.80 to the US dollar by a symbolic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a continuous strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  ECONOMIC JOURNAL  \par \par \uc2 - Ernst & Young says the demand in listing processes of large-sized mainland corporation for accounting manpower has led to a shortage of such personnel in Hong Kong and that may actually affect the listing plans of other companies.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG DAILY NEWS  \par \par \uc2 - Hundreds of people formed a queue outside a half-century old bakery for its products on its last day of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday. The bakery ,forced out by a sharp rent increase, is famous for its Hong Kong-styled egg tarts, which were former governor Chris Patten's favourite.  \par \par \uc2 SING PAO  \par \par \uc2 - The Hang Seng Index fell to its one-month low yesterday, losing 157 points to close at 13,562 on turnover of HK$14.8 billion on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 WEN WEI PO  \par \par \uc2 - Shenhua Energy targets to sell 3.06 billion H shares at a price of HK$7.25 to HK$9.25 each to raise between US$2.8 billion to US$3.6 billion in its planned IPO next month, according to market sources. The Anglo Amercian group is said to be subscribing to US$150 million worth of shares.  \par \par \uc2 TA KUNG PAO  \par \par \uc2 - Upcoming fundraising plans by three new mainland candidates in the Hong Kong stock market this summer target a total of HK$62.9 billion in raised funds.  \par \par \uc2 For mainland China newspapers, doubleclick on [PRESS/CN]  \par \par \uc2 For Shanghai newspapers, see.............[PRESS/CNS]  \par \par \uc2 For Taiwan newspapers, see.[PRESS/TW]  \par \par \uc2 HONGKONG-PRESSDIGEST|LANGEN|G|RBN|Z|REVU|RNP|DNP|PGE|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050526e15q00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
