\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Damp HKD Rise A Week After Cap  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Raymond Tsoi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2005\par 12:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$544 million Wednesday to damp the Hong Kong dollar's rise, just one week after it set a ceiling on the local unit to discourage inflows related to yuan speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said strong foreign interest in coming initial public offerings also helped boost demand for the Hong Kong dollar, which traders have been buying in anticipation of a revaluation of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Although the HKMA's introduction of new convertibility rules last week immediately drove over HK$3 billion of funds away from the territory, speculators soon took the HKMA's move as a sign it has information about an imminent yuan regime change.  \par \par \uc2 "Since China has promised more yuan flexibility over the longer term when it's ready, yuan speculation has persisted," a trader at a local bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign interest in IPOs added further upward pressure to the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Communications, China Shenhua Energy Co. and COSCO Holdings are expected to raise a total of about HK$50 billion in the coming months.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was quoted at around HK$7.7750 Wednesday before the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with around HK$7.7995 before the ceiling of HK$7.750 was announced.  \par \par \uc2 But traders were divided about the near term outlook for the local unit, with some betting on continued strength.  \par \par \uc2 "The rising trend of the Hong Kong dollar will likely continue, as hot money flows in for IPOs and yuan speculation," said DBS strategist Daniel Chan.  \par \par \uc2 Speculators may continue to push the Hong Kong dollar toward the HK$7.75 ceiling set by the HKMA to test how firmly it will back its words, the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Others said they believe Wednesday's HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although small in size, sent a clear signal to the market that it means business.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has responded quite a bit already to the move," said Chordio Chan, head of risk treasury at Citigroup, referring to the U.S. dollar's rise to HK$7.7800 from HK$7.7750 following the HKMA action.  \par \par \uc2 In the short term, the Hong Kong dollar's rise has been capped, and should remain at current levels, unless a significant amount of hot money flows in, Chan added.  \par \par \uc2 Over the longer term, most agree the HKMA has the willingness and ability to stop the Hong Kong dollar from rising beyond the HK$7.75 cap.  \par \par \uc2 -By Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; raymond.tsoi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan [ 25-05-05 1013GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050525e15p000jf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
