\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Dollar Higher In Rebound From Wednesday's Decline  \b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 19, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 By Steven Vames  \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is up modestly in early New York trading, rebounding as traders took Wednesday's weakness as an opportunity to buy dollars Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had fallen Wednesday, mostly due to a report which showed soft U.S. core consumer inflation, which was seen as a sign that Federal Reserve policymakers will have no need to speed up their pace of interest rate tightening this year. Still, the Fed is expected to continue raising rates at a "measured" pace, which gives an advantage to the dollar as rates stagnate in Europe and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Additionally, the dollar was helped by the fact that Hong Kong's introduction Wednesday of a top end to the Hong Kong dollar's trading band, and the debut Wednesday of trading for eight additional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in China, were not harbingers of an imminent revaluation of the Chinese yuan, as some had thought.  \par \par \uc2 Similarly, the Bank of Korea denied the thrust of a report in the Financial Times Wednesday that had suggested that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves are sufficient and that it will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The news from \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China and Korea all help, at least temporarily, to put the issue over China's revaluation into the background. A revaluation from China is widely expected to pressure the dollar versus Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has been the focus of intense speculation and nervousness in markets recently.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's overnight gains were extended early in the U.S. session Thursday after a report indicated that jobless claims fell by 20,000 to 321,000 in the week ended May 14, reversing the increases of the prior two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The figures suggest that the labor market is continuing to strengthen, bolstering the posture of the U.S. economy versus some of its major trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 In early New York trading Thursday, the euro is at $1.2629, down from $1.2677 late Wednesday. The dollar is at Y107.35, up from Y106.86, and at CHF1.2214 from CHF1.2176. The pound is at $1.8366, from $1.8400 and the euro is at Y135.65, up from Y135.44.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 05-19-05 0900ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050519e15j000x2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
